[
  {
    "id": "P0001",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Found findings: 1 High, 4 Medium, and 2 Low**. The requested baseline checks passed, but five local regression tests exposed behavior the existing suite does not catch. No release source files were changed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0002",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Early Hints on a pushed response causes a library panic\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0003",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   [src/proto/streams/state.rs:162](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/state.rs:162), [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:171](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:171).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0004",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Expected: a pushed response may send `103 Early Hints` before its final response.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0005",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: receiving 103 leaves the stream in `ReservedRemote`, although it has already been counted. The final response counts it again and panics at `src/proto/streams/counts.rs:111`: `assertion failed: !stream.is_counted`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0006",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: an ordinary client connection task panics while processing a valid response sequence.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0007",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Evidence: executed `pushed_get_accepts_early_hints_before_final_response`, using a promised GET followed by 103 and 200. Source inspection confirms the assertion is unconditional.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0008",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   transition out of the reserved state on the first response HEADERS while continuing to await final headers; count the stream once.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0009",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Discarded DATA padding is omitted from flow control\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0010",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0011",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Expected: DATA flow control accounts for the entire frame payload, including padding and its length byte. Discarded padding capacity must become reusable.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0012",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: accounting uses only the body length after padding has been stripped.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0013",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: a conforming peer can exhaust its window and stall, even after the application consumes and releases all body data.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0014",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Evidence: executed `padded_data_returns_discarded_padding_capacity`. A 16-byte stream window was filled with one body byte, one padding-length byte, and fourteen padding bytes. After releasing the body byte, no WINDOW_UPDATE arrived within 100 ms. The equivalent unpadded control passed. Source inspection confirms padding never reaches the receive accounting.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0015",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   account for all flow-controlled octets and automatically release discarded padding at both stream and connection levels.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0016",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Reading an empty body consumes and loses its trailing headers. src/proto/streams/recv.rs:1116. When a response consists of initial HEADERS followed directly by trailing HEADERS with END_STREAM, RecvStream::data() pops the next receive event while looking for DATA. Its non-DATA branch returns None and consumes the trailer event. Consequently a normal caller that finishes the body loop before calling trailers() gets Ok(None), losing application metadata such as x-result: ok on responses that contain no DATA frames.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0017",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "A valid 304 response fails when ended with empty DATA\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0018",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:187](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:187), [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:641](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:641).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0019",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Expected: a 304 response may carry a representation `Content-Length` without sending that content, including when an empty DATA frame ends the stream.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0020",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: the length is retained as outstanding body data; the empty final DATA frame produces `PROTOCOL_ERROR`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0021",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: an ordinary conditional GET can fail depending on how the server terminates its response.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0022",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Evidence: executed `not_modified_accepts_empty_end_data` with status 304, `Content-Length: 100`, and empty final DATA. It failed. Ending the same response in HEADERS passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0023",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   represent 304\u2019s no-content semantics throughout body validation, rather than exempting only END_STREAM HEADERS.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0024",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "A 4 GiB capacity request silently becomes zero\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0025",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   [src/share.rs:287](/work/crate/src/share.rs:287).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0026",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Expected: `reserve_capacity(usize)` preserves a positive request or applies a documented limit.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0027",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: casting to `WindowSize` truncates the argument. On this 64-bit host, `reserve_capacity(1usize << 32)` becomes `reserve_capacity(0)`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0028",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: an uploader reserving its remaining file size can cancel its reservation and wait indefinitely for capacity.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0029",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Evidence: executed `reserve_large_body_does_not_cancel_reservation`. An existing positive reservation became zero. Controls requesting 65,535 bytes, 2 GiB, and 4 GiB minus one retained available capacity.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0030",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   clamp before conversion or use a checked conversion with a documented policy for large requests.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0031",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Pushed HEAD responses reject valid representation lengths\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0032",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:749](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:749).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0033",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Expected: pushed HEAD responses receive the same no-body treatment as directly requested HEAD responses. The crate explicitly permits HEAD promises.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0034",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: promise processing never sets `ContentLength::Head`. A 200 response with `Content-Length: 100` and END_STREAM is rejected with `PROTOCOL_ERROR`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0035",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: clients cannot receive otherwise valid pushed HEAD metadata.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0036",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Evidence: executed `pushed_head_accepts_representation_content_length`. Controls using a directly requested HEAD, or a pushed HEAD with length zero, passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0037",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   initialize the promised stream\u2019s content-length state from its request method.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0038",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Strict Clippy fails on the supplied toolchain\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0039",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Locations include [src/hpack/huffman/table.rs:265](/work/crate/src/hpack/huffman/table.rs:265), [src/hpack/table.rs:309](/work/crate/src/hpack/table.rs:309), and [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:160](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:160).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0040",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Expected: the strict lint gate completes successfully.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0041",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: Rust 1.99.1 reports eight errors: a large const array, two Option replacements, manual slice filling, two simplifiable matches, a large error variant, and a collapsible match.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0042",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: maintainers cannot use the requested strict gate successfully. These are pre-existing lint failures, not evidence of a runtime defect. The repository\u2019s Clippy CI job is commented out; older-toolchain behavior was not tested.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0043",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   address the diagnostics or document justified exceptions consistent with the MSRV.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0044",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Documentation checks fail, and panic preconditions are incomplete\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0045",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Locations: [src/hpack/decoder.rs:508](/work/crate/src/hpack/decoder.rs:508), [src/frame/headers.rs:959](/work/crate/src/frame/headers.rs:959), and [src/client.rs:1388](/work/crate/src/client.rs:1388).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0046",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Observed: strict documentation including private items fails on two bare URLs. The advisory pass also reports 24 missing `# Errors` sections and seven missing `# Panics` sections across the all-feature API.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0047",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   A concrete caller-facing omission is that client and server window setters panic above `2^31 \u2212 1`, without documenting that bound or panic. This includes setters returning `Result`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0048",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   Consequence: strict internal documentation builds fail, and callers lack complete configuration preconditions. Default public documentation passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0049",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "   format the URLs and document actual error conditions, bounds, and panics.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0050",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Verification used offline dependencies and `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`, on aarch64 Linux with Rust 1.99.1 unless specified:\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0051",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "- `cargo build --locked` \u2014 passed.\n- `cargo test --locked -p h2 --lib` \u2014 419 passed, one ignored.\n- `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests` \u2014 173 passed, twelve ignored; stress test completed in about 199 seconds.\n- `bash /work/[tooling]/scripts/[tooling] --audit /work/crate` \u2014 exited 1:\n  - `cargo fmt --all --check` \u2014 passed.\n  - `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` \u2014 failed with the eight diagnostics above.\n  - `RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --document-private-items --all-features` \u2014 failed on two bare URLs.\n  - `cargo test --workspace --all-features` \u2014 631 passed, fourteen ignored, including doctests.\n- `cargo build --locked --no-default-features` \u2014 passed.\n- `RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --locked -p h2 --no-deps` \u2014 passed.\n- `python3 -I /work/[tooling]/scripts/[tooling] /work/crate` \u2014 completed; heuristic hits were inspected rather than treated as automatic defects.\n- `cargo +nightly-2026-10-01 miri test --locked -p h2 --lib hpack::encoder::test::test_encode_method` \u2014 three passed.\n- The selected HPACK fixture initially encountered Miri\u2019s filesystem-isolation restriction. Re-running with `MIRIFLAGS=-Zmiri-disable-isolation` passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0052",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Regression tests remain in [the scratch harness](/work/tmp/review-harness/tests/contracts.rs). From `/work/tmp/review-harness`, `cargo test --locked --test contracts` produced five expected failures and five passing controls. Harness formatting and strict Clippy passed. Its existing `h2-support` dependency enables `stream` and `unstable`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0053",
    "report": "A",
    "text": "Final count: findings. Coverage combined source inspection with ordinary local regression tests; no dedicated security assessment was performed. Rust 1.63 compatibility, other platforms, full-suite Miri, and h2spec were not run. Last clock reading: **1,070.3 of 2,700 seconds \u2014 17.835 minutes**."
  },
  {
    "id": "P0054",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Found **8 issues: 6 behavioral defects, 1 documentation defect, and 1 lint failure**. The release source was left unchanged.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0055",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Queued uploads can deadlock active requests \u2014 [src/proto/streams/prioritize.rs:287](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/prioritize.rs:287).  \n   Capacity reservation should allow active streams to progress while another stream waits for the concurrency limit. Instead, `reserve_capacity` assigns connection capacity to a stream in `pending_open`. With `MAX_CONCURRENT_STREAMS=1`, a queued upload that reserves and buffers 65,535 bytes consumes the entire connection window. The active request cannot finish, so the queued request cannot open.  \n   **Executed evidence:** `queued_reservation_must_not_block_active_request` timed out after one second. The otherwise identical upload without explicit reservation passed. Both calls to `send_request` followed successful readiness checks.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0056",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Padded DATA can stall a conforming peer \u2014 [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580).  \n   HTTP/2 flow control counts the complete DATA payload, including padding. This code counts only the application payload after padding has been stripped. With a five-byte receive window, a valid frame containing one data byte, the padding-length byte, and three padding bytes exhausts the peer\u2019s window. Releasing the application byte produces no window update, leaving both sides waiting.  \n   **Executed evidence:** `padded_receive_window_replenishes` timed out; its unpadded control passed. **Source inspection:** the existing `data_padding` test checks payload stripping on an ended stream, so it cannot detect this replenishment failure.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0057",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Zero-byte writes can cause an endless polling loop \u2014 [src/codec/framed_write.rs:137](/work/crate/src/codec/framed_write.rs:137).  \n   An `AsyncWrite` returning `Ok(0)` for nonempty input should terminate the write with `WriteZero`. Here, the returned byte count is discarded and the unchanged buffer is retried immediately. A permanently exhausted transport therefore prevents the poll from returning and can block its executor thread.  \n   **Executed evidence:** a bounded writer returned `Ok(0)` once, then a distinct error on any retry. Server handshake called it twice and returned the second error instead of `WriteZero`. The endless-loop consequence follows from source inspection; an unbounded spinning test was not run.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0058",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Buffered request DATA bypasses a zero peer stream window. src/proto/streams/prioritize.rs:216. After the client has processed SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE=0, SendStream::send_data still schedules nonempty DATA using the available connection window. For example, sending a three-byte POST body with end_of_stream=true emits that DATA before the peer grants any stream credit. The scheduler fails to enforce the zero stream window, so the client violates the peer's advertised flow-control state even on a small request.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0059",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "A 4 GiB capacity reservation silently becomes zero \u2014 [src/share.rs:287](/work/crate/src/share.rs:287).  \n   `reserve_capacity` accepts `usize` without documenting a size restriction, but casts it directly to `u32`. On this 64-bit platform, `reserve_capacity(1_usize << 32)` becomes a cancellation of the reservation. Callers reserving from a large file\u2019s remaining length can consequently wait indefinitely for capacity.  \n   **Executed evidence:** `reservation_larger_than_u32_must_not_become_zero` observed capacity fall from 65,535 to zero without sending data. Large requests should be bounded internally or explicitly rejected, rather than silently wrapping.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0060",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "A second outstanding user PING overwrites the first instead of failing. src/share.rs:556. Calling PingPong::send_ping twice before polling the first pong succeeds twice because the public wrapper does not check whether a user ping is already outstanding. The second call replaces the pending state for the first operation instead of returning a user error, so the caller can no longer reliably complete the original ping through poll_pong.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0061",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Valid 304 responses fail when terminated by empty DATA \u2014 [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:187](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:187).  \n   A 304 response may advertise the selected representation\u2019s nonzero Content-Length while carrying no content. The implementation exempts a 304 ending in HEADERS from length matching, but retains `ContentLength::Remaining` when END_STREAM arrives later. The empty DATA frame then fails the length check at [recv.rs:641](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:641).  \n   **Executed evidence:** a conditional GET receiving `304`, `content-length: 123`, and empty DATA with END_STREAM yielded a body error containing `PROTOCOL_ERROR`. The same response ending in HEADERS passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0062",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Pushed HEAD responses lose HEAD semantics \u2014 [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:749](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:749).  \n   HEAD responses must permit nonzero Content-Length without requiring corresponding body bytes. Ordinary HEAD requests set `ContentLength::Head`; received HEAD push promises never set that state. Consequently, a valid pushed HEAD response is rejected as truncated.  \n   **Executed evidence:** `pushed_head_with_content_length` received a library-generated `PROTOCOL_ERROR` for a headers-only response with `content-length: 123`. The ordinary HEAD control passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0063",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "The server guide names a removed connection-driving method \u2014 [src/server.rs:40](/work/crate/src/server.rs:40).  \n   The guide repeatedly instructs callers to use `Connection::poll_close`, while the public method is `poll_closed`. Following this guidance when driving a connection without accepting requests fails to compile. The same guide also incorrectly says graceful shutdown is unimplemented at [server.rs:57](/work/crate/src/server.rs:57), despite the public `graceful_shutdown` method.  \n   **Executed evidence:** a scratch example using `poll_close` failed with E0599; changing it to `poll_closed` compiled successfully.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0064",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Clippy cannot complete the library test target on the supplied toolchain \u2014 [src/lib.rs:88](/work/crate/src/lib.rs:88).  \n   Under Clippy 0.1.99, `cfg_attr(test, deny(warnings))` turns eight existing Clippy warnings into errors. These include the large constant array at `src/hpack/huffman/table.rs:265` and `mem_replace_option_with_some` at `src/hpack/table.rs:309`. This blocks the full lint check even though compilation and tests pass.  \n   **Executed evidence:** `cargo clippy --locked -p h2 --all-targets --all-features` exited 101 on the unmodified release. This is a toolchain-specific lint failure, not evidence of a runtime defect.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0065",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "All commands used `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`, Rust **1.99.1**, and prefetched locked dependencies on **aarch64 Linux**. Checks ran from `/work/crate` unless noted.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0066",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Unknown SETTINGS identifiers abort an otherwise valid handshake. src/frame/settings.rs:160. Settings decoding treats an unrecognized identifier as an invalid setting rather than ignoring it. If a server's initial SETTINGS frame contains the six-byte entry identifier 0x0abc, value 7, the client closes the connection instead of acknowledging the frame. This prevents an ordinary GET from completing against a peer that advertises an extension setting, despite the requirement to ignore unknown SETTINGS parameters.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0067",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "103 Early Hints resolves the final response future prematurely. src/proto/streams/recv.rs:249. A server that sends HEADERS with :status=103 and later sends its final 200 response causes the client ResponseFuture to complete with the 103 message. The receive path queues the first response HEADERS as the application response without filtering informational statuses, so a caller cannot obtain the final status and headers through the ordinary response API.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0068",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "| Command | Outcome |\n|---|---|\n| `cargo build --locked` | Passed |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2 --lib` | 419 passed, 1 ignored |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests` | 173 passed, 12 ignored; stress test passed in 204.18 seconds |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2 --lib --all-features` | 419 passed, 1 ignored |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2 --doc --all-features` | 39 passed, 1 ignored |\n| `cargo doc --locked -p h2 --no-deps --features stream` | Passed |\n| `cargo fmt --all --check` | Passed |\n| `cargo clippy --locked -p h2 --all-targets --all-features` | Failed as described above |\n| `RUSTFLAGS=-Dwarnings cargo test --locked -p h2-tests --no-run` | Pre-existing failure: four deprecated `std::usize` usages in `tests/h2-support/src/mock.rs` |\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0069",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "The scratch copy is `/work/tmp/review`. Its only additions are [review_regressions.rs](/work/tmp/review/tests/h2-tests/tests/review_regressions.rs) and two documentation-check examples. No implementation or existing test was edited.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0070",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "From that copy, `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests --test review_regressions -- --nocapture --test-threads=1` produced **6 expected regression failures and 4 passing controls**. `cargo check --locked -p h2 --example review_doc_api` failed with E0599; the corrected `review_doc_api_current` example passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0071",
    "report": "B",
    "text": "Total: findings. Coverage was source inspection plus existing and targeted local tests, without exhaustive path coverage. MSRV 1.63 was unavailable and untested. Release-profile tests, h2spec, Miri, fuzzing, and dedicated security testing were not run. Last clock reading: **16.001866666666665 minutes used of 45.0**."
  },
  {
    "id": "P0072",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "The review found **5 Medium and findings. The requested baseline build and tests pass, but six focused local regressions demonstrate behavior or error-handling problems. No production files were edited; regression harnesses are retained in `/work/tmp/probes`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0073",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Medium\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0074",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "DATA padding consumes flow-control credit without returning it\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0075",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "[src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0076",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "HTTP/2 flow control counts the entire DATA payload, including the padding-length octet and padding. This code counts only the application payload after padding has been stripped.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0077",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Executed evidence: with a 256-byte stream window, a legal DATA frame containing one padding-length octet and 255 padding octets produced an empty application buffer. h2 reported available capacity of 256 and used capacity of zero, and sent no WINDOW_UPDATE within the test\u2019s 200 ms timeout. The peer has exhausted its window and cannot continue the body.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0078",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "account for the original DATA payload length and automatically release discarded padding on both stream and connection windows. The existing `data_padding` test verifies body contents without checking credit accounting.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0079",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Regression: [padding.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/padding.rs).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0080",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Oversized trailers silently lose fields\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0081",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "[src/proto/streams/recv.rs:361](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:361).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0082",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Header decoding marks oversized blocks and omits fields beyond the configured limit. Initial-header handling checks that marker; trailer handling ignores it and delivers the remaining fields successfully.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0083",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Executed evidence: with `max_header_list_size(64)`, trailers containing `checksum` with a 100-byte value returned `Ok(Some({}))`. A caller receives successful completion with missing metadata.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0084",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "reject oversized trailers before delivering them, consistently with oversized initial headers.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0085",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Regression: [trailers.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/trailers.rs).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0086",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "A response without `:status` becomes HTTP 200\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0087",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "[src/client.rs:1676](/work/crate/src/client.rs:1676).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0088",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "A response must contain the mandatory `:status` pseudo-header. When it is absent, this conversion leaves `http::Response::builder()` at its default status.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0089",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Executed evidence: a response header block containing only `content-length: 0` and END_STREAM returned a successful HTTP 200 response. Callers can therefore mistake a malformed response for success.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0090",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "require `pseudo.status` and return a stream protocol error when absent.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0091",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Regression: [status.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/status.rs).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0092",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Large capacity reservations wrap to zero\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0093",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "[src/share.rs:287](/work/crate/src/share.rs:287).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0094",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "`reserve_capacity` accepts `usize` but casts directly to the `u32` window-size type. Its documentation specifies no corresponding argument limit.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0095",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Executed evidence: reservations of 1,024 and 4,294,967,295 bytes received capacity; 4,294,967,296 received zero, and 4,294,967,297 received one byte. Reserving a large remaining file size requires no large allocation, so an ordinary streaming caller can encounter this boundary and wait indefinitely for capacity.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0096",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "retain the demand as `usize`, or clamp it before narrowing.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0097",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Regression: [capacity.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/capacity.rs).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0098",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Header lists exactly at the advertised limit are rejected\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0099",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Locations: [src/frame/headers.rs:869](/work/crate/src/frame/headers.rs:869), [src/frame/headers.rs:911](/work/crate/src/frame/headers.rs:911).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0100",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Both comparisons use `< max_header_list_size`, rejecting equality despite the documented maximum.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0101",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Executed evidence: `:status: 200` occupies 42 decoded header-list bytes. It succeeded with limit 43 and failed with `PROTOCOL_ERROR` at limit 42. Adding `x: a` produced the same result at 77 versus its exact size of 76.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0102",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Consequently, a peer respecting the advertised maximum can have a valid response rejected.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0103",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "accept equality in both comparisons and add boundary regressions.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0104",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Regression: [headers.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/headers.rs).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0105",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Low\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0106",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Connection errors discard underlying I/O details\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0107",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Locations: [src/proto/error.rs:92](/work/crate/src/proto/error.rs:92), [src/error.rs:130](/work/crate/src/error.rs:130).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0108",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "The internal conversion retains the I/O kind and optionally a string, then reconstructs an `io::Error`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0109",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Executed evidence: a mock transport returning OS error 5 yielded an h2 error whose `get_io().raw_os_error()` was `None`. Source inspection also shows typed inner errors becoming strings, while `Error::source()` is not implemented.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0110",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Callers lose diagnostic details and cannot reliably classify underlying failures.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0111",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "preserve OS codes and typed causes through the cloneable internal representation, and expose the underlying cause through `source()`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0112",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Regression: [io_error.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/io_error.rs).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0113",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Documentation omits panic contracts and fails the private-item check\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0114",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Locations include [src/client.rs:1388](/work/crate/src/client.rs:1388), [src/server.rs:481](/work/crate/src/server.rs:481), [src/hpack/decoder.rs:508](/work/crate/src/hpack/decoder.rs:508), and [src/frame/headers.rs:959](/work/crate/src/frame/headers.rs:959).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0115",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "The audit reported **24 missing `# Errors` sections and seven missing `# Panics` sections**, including four stable connection window setters that assert a 31-bit size bound without documenting it. This makes configuration APIs harder to use correctly.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0116",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "The strict private-item rustdoc command also failed on two bare URLs. Public documentation built successfully.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0117",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "document the applicable error and panic conditions, especially window bounds, and mark the two URLs as links.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0118",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Strict Clippy fails on the supplied toolchain\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0119",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Locations include [src/hpack/huffman/table.rs:265](/work/crate/src/hpack/huffman/table.rs:265), [src/hpack/table.rs:309](/work/crate/src/hpack/table.rs:309), and [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:160](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:160).\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0120",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "The warnings-denied Clippy check stopped on eight diagnostics: one large constant array, two `Option` replacements, one manual slice fill, two question-mark suggestions, one large error variant, and one collapsible match.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0121",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "These are pre-existing strict-check failures, not demonstrated runtime defects. Fix the diagnostics while preserving the declared MSRV. No historical toolchain was available to establish which diagnostics represent toolchain drift.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0122",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Verification\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0123",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Commands ran offline with `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`, using Rust/Cargo **1.99.1** on aarch64 Linux.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0124",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "From `/work/crate`:\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0125",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "- `cargo build --locked` \u2014 **passed**.\n- `cargo test --locked -p h2 --lib` \u2014 **419 passed, one ignored**.\n- `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests` \u2014 **173 passed, 12 ignored**, including the stress test.\n- `bash /work/[tooling]/scripts/[tooling] --audit /work/crate` \u2014 formatting and all-feature workspace tests **passed**; strict Clippy and private-item rustdoc **failed** as described above.\n- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` \u2014 **failed**, eight h2 diagnostics.\n- `RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --document-private-items --all-features` \u2014 **failed**, two bare URLs.\n- `cargo test --workspace --all-features` \u2014 **passed**, including 39 passing doctests.\n- `RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --locked -p h2 --no-deps --all-features` \u2014 **passed**.\n- `cargo build --locked --no-default-features` \u2014 **passed**.\n- `cargo check --locked -p h2 --features stream` and `cargo check --locked -p h2 --features unstable` \u2014 **passed**.\n- `RUSTFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo test --locked -p h2-tests --no-run` \u2014 **failed before tests ran**, on four pre-existing deprecation diagnostics in `tests/h2-support/src/mock.rs`. This reproduces the repository CI\u2019s warnings-as-errors configuration.\n- `cargo +nightly-2026-10-01 miri test --locked -p h2 --lib hpack::decoder::test::test_decode_continuation_header_with_non_huff_encoded_name` \u2014 **one targeted test passed**.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0126",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "The heuristic scan completed; style-only hits without a concrete consequence were not promoted to findings.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0127",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "From `/work/tmp/probes`, `cargo test --locked --no-fail-fast -- --nocapture` ran all six regression targets; **all six failed as described above**. Harness formatting and warnings-denied Clippy checks passed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0128",
    "report": "C",
    "text": "Coverage was focused rather than exhaustive across protocol interleavings. Full Miri, Rust 1.63/MSRV, cross-platform, and h2spec checks were not run. No dedicated security testing was performed. Last `/work/clock` reading: **19.35205 of 45 minutes used**."
  },
  {
    "id": "P0129",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "Found **six correctness defects and one pre-existing Clippy failure**. The requested baseline build and tests pass.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0130",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "Pushed responses with Early Hints panic.  \n   [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:171](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:171), [counts.rs:111](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/counts.rs:111)  \n   A pushed response containing `103` followed by final `200` should deliver the final response. Instead, both header blocks increment the stream count, triggering `assert!(!stream.is_counted)`. This panics the caller\u2019s connection task. The scratch regression `pushed_response_allows_early_hints` reproduced this in **debug and optimized release builds**. Existing Early Hints tests cover ordinary responses.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0131",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "Padded DATA loses flow-control credit and can stall responses.  \n   [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580)  \n   HTTP/2 flow control counts the complete DATA payload, including padding. The implementation counts only the application bytes remaining after padding removal. In `padded_data_restores_progress`, one valid frame exhausts a 16-byte stream window: one application byte, one padding-length byte and fourteen padding bytes. After the application releases its byte, no `WINDOW_UPDATE` arrives within one second, preventing the peer from continuing. The equivalent unpadded control passes. Source inspection shows the same shortened size feeds connection accounting. The existing padding test checks body extraction after END_STREAM, missing this failure.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0132",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "Zero-byte transport writes are retried without detecting failure.  \n   [src/codec/framed_write.rs:137](/work/crate/src/codec/framed_write.rs:137)  \n   A successful zero-byte write of a nonempty buffer should produce `WriteZero`, allowing connection error handling to proceed. Instead, the flush loop retries immediately without consuming bytes. Bounded tests used a writer returning zero once and a sentinel error on its second call: both codec flushing and public `server::handshake` returned the second error after two writes. Source inspection establishes that a writer continuing to return zero would keep this loop running without yielding; that unbounded case was not executed.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0133",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "Valid 304 responses fail when terminated by empty DATA.  \n   [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:187](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:187), [recv.rs:641](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:641)  \n   A 304 may carry a nonzero `Content-Length` describing the selected representation while sending no content. The implementation exempts an END_STREAM header block, but retains `Remaining(length)` and rejects a subsequent empty END_STREAM DATA frame. `not_modified_ends_in_empty_data` receives a library `PROTOCOL_ERROR`; the otherwise identical response ending in HEADERS passes. Ordinary cache revalidation can therefore fail depending on framing.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0134",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "A 4 GiB capacity reservation silently becomes cancellation.  \n   [src/share.rs:285](/work/crate/src/share.rs:285)  \n   `reserve_capacity(usize)` documents a total-byte reservation without this upper limit. Its unchecked conversion to `u32` turns exactly 4 GiB into zero. On this 64-bit platform, `reserve_capacity_four_gib` first obtains 65,535 bytes, then observes capacity fall to zero after requesting 4 GiB. A caller reserving the remaining size of a large upload can consequently wait for capacity that was actually cancelled. No large allocation was needed to reproduce this.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0135",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "Rejected push requests leave unreachable stream state behind.  \n   [src/proto/streams/streams.rs:1209](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/streams.rs:1209)  \n   Failed `push_request` validation should release its provisional stream. Instead, `convert_push_message(...)?` returns before the cleanup below it, after inserting the reserved child stream. Rejecting one POST push increases stored streams from one to two; a second regression confirms the extra stream remains after the parent response completes and the next request arrives. Repeated recoverable validation errors therefore retain state for the connection\u2019s lifetime. A connection-header rejection control exercises the later cleanup path and passes.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0136",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "All-target Clippy fails on Rust 1.99.1.  \n   [src/lib.rs:88](/work/crate/src/lib.rs:88)  \n   `cargo clippy --locked -p h2 --all-targets --all-features` exits 101 because the library-test target\u2019s `deny(warnings)` promotes eight Clippy diagnostics to errors. These include `large_const_arrays`, `mem_replace_option_with_some`, `manual_slice_fill`, `question_mark`, `result_large_err` and `collapsible_match`. Maintainers cannot complete this lint configuration without fixes or allowances. This is a pre-existing lint failure; ordinary compilation succeeds, and Clippy is commented out in the supplied CI configuration.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0137",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "All commands below used `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`, Rust **1.99.1**, and the supplied lockfile on aarch64 Linux. Checks ran from `/work/crate` unless indicated.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0138",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "| Command | Outcome |\n|---|---|\n| `cargo build --locked` | Passed |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2 --lib` | 419 passed, 1 ignored |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests` | 173 passed, 12 ignored; stress test passed in 205.82 seconds |\n| `cargo fmt --all --check` | Passed |\n| `cargo check --locked -p h2 --all-targets --all-features` | Passed |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2 --lib --all-features` | 419 passed, 1 ignored |\n| `RUSTFLAGS=-Dwarnings cargo build --locked -p h2 --all-features` | Passed |\n| `cargo clippy --locked -p h2 --all-targets --all-features` | Failed as described above |\n| `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-Dwarnings cargo doc --locked -p h2 --all-features --no-deps` | Passed |\n| `cargo test --locked -p h2 --doc --all-features` | 39 passed, 1 ignored |\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0139",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "In `/work/tmp/review`, `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests --test review_regressions -- --nocapture` compiled and ran **11 tests: three controls passed and eight tests failed**, covering the six correctness defects. The release command, `cargo test --locked -p h2-tests --release --test review_regressions pushed_response_allows_early_hints -- --nocapture`, also reproduced the panic.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0140",
    "report": "D",
    "text": "The [scratch regression file](/work/tmp/review/tests/h2-tests/tests/review_regressions.rs) is the only addition to that copy. No production fixes were made. Hash verification confirmed every original release file remained unchanged. Integration checks also emitted four pre-existing deprecation warnings from `h2-support`.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "P0141",
